**Answer: Yes.** $\|1_A - b_n^{-1/2}\| \to 0$.

**Proof.**

Let $\varepsilon_n := \|1_A - b_n\|$. By hypothesis $\varepsilon_n \to 0$, so for all sufficiently large $n$ we have $\varepsilon_n < 1$.

Since each $b_n$ is positive, its spectrum is real and
$$\sigma(1_A - b_n) \subseteq [-\varepsilon_n, \varepsilon_n],$$
hence
$$\sigma(b_n) \subseteq [1-\varepsilon_n,\, 1+\varepsilon_n].$$
For $\varepsilon_n < 1$ this interval lies in $(0,\infty)$, confirming $b_n$ is invertible (already given).

Apply the continuous functional calculus with $f(t) = t^{-1/2}$, which is continuous on $(0,\infty)$. Then $b_n^{-1/2} = f(b_n)$ is positive (hence self-adjoint), and
$$\sigma(b_n^{-1/2}) = f(\sigma(b_n)) \subseteq \bigl[(1+\varepsilon_n)^{-1/2},\,(1-\varepsilon_n)^{-1/2}\bigr].$$

Since $b_n^{-1/2}$ is self-adjoint, so is $1_A - b_n^{-1/2}$, and the norm of a self-adjoint element equals its spectral radius:
$$\|1_A - b_n^{-1/2}\| = \sup_{\mu \in \sigma(b_n^{-1/2})} |1 - \mu|.$$

The interval $\bigl[(1+\varepsilon_n)^{-1/2},\,(1-\varepsilon_n)^{-1/2}\bigr]$ contains $1$, so the supremum of $|1-\mu|$ over it is attained at an endpoint:
$$\|1_A - b_n^{-1/2}\| \le \max\Bigl\{(1-\varepsilon_n)^{-1/2}-1,\;\; 1-(1+\varepsilon_n)^{-1/2}\Bigr\}.$$

Both summands tend to $0$ as $\varepsilon_n \to 0$ (by continuity of $t \mapsto t^{-1/2}$ at $t=1$). Therefore
$$\|1_A - b_n^{-1/2}\| \to 0. \qquad \blacksquare$$

